# Claude Code local state (worktrees, scheduled-task locks, etc.).
# Nothing under this directory is source — it's all per-checkout tooling
# artifacts that would pollute the repo if committed.
.claude/

# Local Docker Compose overrides
docker-compose.override.yml

# Local scratch files
.local/


# Bun leaves a multi-megabyte intermediate here when `bun build --compile` is
# interrupted. One reached a branch once (SC-94); a 61 MB blob in git history is
# permanent and everyone pays to clone it forever.
*.bun-build

# Two tests under `scripts/tests/` rewrite tracked source in place and journal
# the original bytes here first, so a killed run is repaired by the next one
# (SC-601). Ignored because the repair for one committable artefact must not
# introduce another; `scripts/check-staged-test-fixtures.ts` refuses a commit
# while one exists.
scripts/.source-mutation-journal-*.json

# Agent scratch. Nothing in this repository creates either of these and no
# contributor here will meet one, which is exactly what makes them read as
# debris a tidy-up should remove (SC-758).
#
# They are written by agent tooling running in a working copy, and an untracked
# directory SURVIVES `git checkout`. So a session that starts on one branch and
# continues on a branch of this tree arrives with the scratch still on disk,
# under a `.gitignore` that does not name it, and the next `git add -A` takes
# it. The rule has to live in the copy the checkout carries; being ignored
# somewhere else is not being ignored.
#
# Measured 2026-08-28, one throwaway repo per copy of this file, identical
# contents, the ignore rules the only variable: `.tmp-thr_abc/notes.md` and
# `.context/handoff.md` were both STAGEABLE under the copy lacking these lines.
# Control: `scani-test-fixture-x.ts` was ignored under both, so that reading is
# the rule missing rather than the probe broken.
#
# `scripts/tests/root-gitignore-covers-orphanable-output.test.ts` pins both --
# by asking git, not by grepping this file, and it asserts that THIS file is
# what answers. A per-clone `.git/info/exclude` satisfies the verdict and is
# not a mechanism: every worktree of one clone honours it and a fresh clone
# does not (SC-681, SC-685).
.tmp-thr_*/
.context/

# Build output that lives beside the app or module producing it, and
# infrastructure working state (SC-569, SC-577).
#
# These belong here rather than in a per-directory `.gitignore` because a
# per-directory one is a TRACKED FILE: any checkout that does not include that
# directory deletes the rule while the artefacts stay on disk, and output that
# was ignored a moment ago becomes an ordinary untracked file the next
# `git add -A` will take. Measured once at eleven files, one of them Terraform
# state.
#
# GLOBS, never paths. A list of directories would be an inventory of what some
# other checkout of this tree contains, which is its own disclosure; `**/.next/`
# names nothing and covers every app that ever adds one.
**/.next/
**/.vercel/
**/.turbo/
**/.wrangler/
**/next-env.d.ts.bak
**/public/version.json

# Terraform working state. `*.tfstate` carries resource identifiers and, for
# several providers, credentials in plaintext.
**/.terraform/
**/*.tfstate
**/*.tfstate.backup
**/tfplan

# Every fixture a test under `scripts/tests/` writes INSIDE this repository
# (SC-609). Those tests must write in place — `check-docs.ts` derives its lists
# from the tree, so a fixture in TMPDIR exercises nothing — and they remove
# them in `afterEach`, which `SIGKILL` skips. Eleven such paths were committable
# when this was added; `git add -A` cannot stage an ignored one, at all, which
# is why this rule and not a longer sweep list.
#
# ONE rule, matched by a reserved name prefix, so the twelfth fixture is covered
# the moment it is named rather than when somebody remembers to widen a glob.
# `scripts/lib/test-fixture-corpses.ts` refuses at write time any in-repo
# fixture this line does not reach — it asks git, so deleting this line turns
# those tests red rather than turning the guard into a no-op.
**/scani-test-fixture-*
